<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
	<!-- MYR-247 / MYR-434 — Apple privacy manifest (required for App Store submission).
	     XcodeGen picks this up as a RESOURCE purely from its position in `App/Sources`
	     (there is no `project.yml` entry for it) and copies it to the app bundle ROOT,
	     which is where Apple looks. The widget extension has its OWN manifest at
	     `Widgets/Sources/PrivacyInfo.xcprivacy` — a manifest is bundle-scoped, so this
	     file does NOT describe the `.appex`. See that file for why its declaration is
	     empty and what that emptiness means.

	     MYR-434 — every type below is DERIVED FROM A CALL SITE, not from a template.
	     `PrivacyManifestTests` pins this list against a constant, so a new collecting
	     code path fails a test instead of silently reintroducing the drift that made
	     this array empty in the first place. The rule that keeps this file true:
	     COLLECTION IS TRANSMISSION. Anything that only ever lives in `UserDefaults`
	     is not collected and is not declared.

	     Everything is `Linked` = true and `Tracking` = false, for one structural
	     reason each. Linked: there is no anonymous mode — every REST call carries the
	     account's bearer token, so nothing reaches us detached from an identity.
	     Tracking: there are no third-party dependencies at all (`project.yml` pins one
	     remote package, our own `contracts`; `MyRoboTaxiKit` and `DesignSystem` add
	     zero), no `AdSupport`/`AppTrackingTransparency`/`ASIdentifierManager` anywhere,
	     no advertising identifier, and no data is shared with a data broker. That is
	     also why `NSPrivacyTrackingDomains` is empty.

	     Type-by-type, with the field that actually crosses the wire:

	       Name / Email — `AppleSignInRequest.fullName` / `.email` on
	         `POST /api/auth/apple` (`AuthSession.signIn` reads them off the
	         `ASAuthorizationAppleIDCredential`; Apple only supplies them on the FIRST
	         authorization, but a first authorization is still a collection).

	       Precise location — `RidePlace.lat`/`.lng` on `POST /api/ride-requests`.
	         The rider's device fix becomes the `pickup` coordinate
	         (`LiveUserLocation` → `SharedViewerState.pinDropCoordinate` →
	         `LiveRideRequestService.createBody`). PRECISE, not coarse: 100 m
	         `desiredAccuracy` is far finer than Apple's ~3 sq km coarse threshold, and
	         the app never requests reduced accuracy. This one string also covers the
	         vehicle's coordinates that `/privacy` discloses — see the note below.

	       User ID — the Apple account identifier (`sub`) inside the identity token,
	         which is how the server recognises a returning signer-in.

	       Device ID — the APNs `deviceToken` on `PUT /api/push/devices` and the
	         ActivityKit `activityToken` on
	         `POST /api/ride-requests/{id}/activity-token`. Device-level identifiers
	         stored against the account; both are deleted on sign-out / ride end.

	       Other user content — free text the user types that we store: the share
	         invite `label` (`CreateShareInviteRequest`), the vehicle `plate`
	         (`PUT /api/tesla/vehicles/{id}/plate`), and the `label`/`address` on
	         `pickup`/`dropoff`.

	       Other data types — vehicle telemetry: VIN, charge, cabin, media, service
	         state and drive history. Declared even though THIS BINARY only ever
	         RECEIVES it (REST snapshot + telemetry WebSocket; no outbound frame
	         carries a coordinate, and `LastKnownVehiclePositionStore` is a local cache
	         only). It is collected FROM TESLA BY OUR SERVER in connection with the
	         app and linked to the account, `/privacy` says so at length, and the
	         nutrition label has to agree with `/privacy`. Under-declaring is the bug
	         MYR-434 exists to fix, so the tie goes to declaring.

	     DELIBERATELY NOT DECLARED — phone number and other-people's contact info.
	     MYR-382 removed the "booked for someone else" passenger UI. The wire fields
	     `RideRequestCreateRequest.passengerName`/`.passengerPhone` still EXIST, but
	     `SharedViewerState.draftPassenger` has no writer anywhere in `App/Sources`
	     (only `App/Tests`), so on a shipping build both are always nil and the
	     optional encoder omits the keys entirely — a user cannot enter a phone number
	     at all. `/privacy` states the same thing in prose ("there is no way to book on
	     someone else's behalf"). If anyone ever re-adds a writer to `draftPassenger`,
	     THIS declaration is what has to change first. A third party's NAME can still
	     be typed into the share invite `label`, and that is covered by
	     `…OtherUserContent` above rather than by a contact-info type.

	     Also absent, verified by grep across `App`, `Widgets` and `Packages`: health,
	     fitness, contacts, photos, camera, microphone, purchases, browsing history,
	     search history (destination search runs through `MKLocalSearchCompleter`, so
	     those terms go to Apple and never to us), sensitive info, analytics and
	     product-interaction data.

	     REQUIRED-REASON APIs — `UserDefaults` is still the only category this app
	     calls, so CA92.1 (access info from the same app) remains the whole story: it
	     holds this app's own session, mode choice, recent destinations, push
	     registration, Live Activity ride ids, dispatch pointer, last-known vehicle
	     position and first-run demo flag, and none of it is shared with another app or
	     developer. There is no App Group. The call sites are far broader than the
	     three this comment used to name — `AccountStorage`, `AuthSession`, `RootView`,
	     `Places/RecentDestinations`, `Push/PushRegistrationStore`,
	     `Push/PushRegistrationCoordinator`, `LiveActivity/RideActivityRideIdentity`,
	     `LiveActivity/RideActivityCoordinator`, `Services/OwnerDispatchPointer`,
	     `Services/LiveRideRequestService`, `Vehicles/LastKnownVehiclePosition`,
	     `Screens/Demo/FirstRunDemo`, `Screens/Shared/SharedViewerState` — which is why
	     the list is described by category here instead of enumerated and left to rot.
	     Still ZERO use of the other three categories: no file-timestamp API
	     (`FileManager` appears in no Swift file in the repo), no system-boot-time API,
	     no disk-space API, no active-keyboard API. -->
	<key>NSPrivacyTracking</key>
	<false/>
	<key>NSPrivacyTrackingDomains</key>
	<array/>
	<key>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypes</key>
	<array>
		<dict>
			<key>NSPrivacyCollectedDataType</key>
			<string>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypeName</string>
			<key>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypeLinked</key>
			<true/>
			<key>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypeTracking</key>
			<false/>
			<key>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypePurposes</key>
			<array>
				<string>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypePurposeAppFunctionality</string>
			</array>
		</dict>
		<dict>
			<key>NSPrivacyCollectedDataType</key>
			<string>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypeEmailAddress</string>
			<key>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypeLinked</key>
			<true/>
			<key>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypeTracking</key>
			<false/>
			<key>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypePurposes</key>
			<array>
				<string>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypePurposeAppFunctionality</string>
			</array>
		</dict>
		<dict>
			<key>NSPrivacyCollectedDataType</key>
			<string>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypePreciseLocation</string>
			<key>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypeLinked</key>
			<true/>
			<key>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypeTracking</key>
			<false/>
			<key>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypePurposes</key>
			<array>
				<string>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypePurposeAppFunctionality</string>
			</array>
		</dict>
		<dict>
			<key>NSPrivacyCollectedDataType</key>
			<string>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypeUserID</string>
			<key>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypeLinked</key>
			<true/>
			<key>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypeTracking</key>
			<false/>
			<key>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypePurposes</key>
			<array>
				<string>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypePurposeAppFunctionality</string>
			</array>
		</dict>
		<dict>
			<key>NSPrivacyCollectedDataType</key>
			<string>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypeDeviceID</string>
			<key>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypeLinked</key>
			<true/>
			<key>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypeTracking</key>
			<false/>
			<key>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypePurposes</key>
			<array>
				<string>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypePurposeAppFunctionality</string>
			</array>
		</dict>
		<dict>
			<key>NSPrivacyCollectedDataType</key>
			<string>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypeOtherUserContent</string>
			<key>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypeLinked</key>
			<true/>
			<key>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypeTracking</key>
			<false/>
			<key>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypePurposes</key>
			<array>
				<string>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypePurposeAppFunctionality</string>
			</array>
		</dict>
		<dict>
			<key>NSPrivacyCollectedDataType</key>
			<string>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypeOtherDataTypes</string>
			<key>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypeLinked</key>
			<true/>
			<key>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypeTracking</key>
			<false/>
			<key>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypePurposes</key>
			<array>
				<string>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypePurposeAppFunctionality</string>
			</array>
		</dict>
	</array>
	<key>NSPrivacyAccessedAPITypes</key>
	<array>
		<dict>
			<key>NSPrivacyAccessedAPIType</key>
			<string>NSPrivacyAccessedAPICategoryUserDefaults</string>
			<key>NSPrivacyAccessedAPITypeReasons</key>
			<array>
				<string>CA92.1</string>
			</array>
		</dict>
	</array>
</dict>
</plist>
